WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.310
I once read a school's AI policy.

2
00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:07.080
Twelve pages, careful and complete,

3
00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:10.100
and not one teacher had read it.

4
00:00:10.700 --> 00:00:12.300
A policy that nobody

5
00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:14.220
follows protects nobody.

6
00:00:15.560 --> 00:00:18.620
Today: an AI security policy on

7
00:00:18.620 --> 00:00:21.140
one page, and how to turn its

8
00:00:21.140 --> 00:00:23.160
rules into checks that hold.

9
00:00:24.440 --> 00:00:26.580
A good AI security policy

10
00:00:26.580 --> 00:00:28.240
fits on one page.

11
00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:31.640
It says which tools are approved, and

12
00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:33.340
how a new one gets approved.

13
00:00:34.030 --> 00:00:35.210
It sets the data rules:

14
00:00:35.690 --> 00:00:37.490
green, amber and red.

15
00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:40.400
It says what agents may do alone,

16
00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:42.960
and what needs a person's yes.

17
00:00:43.750 --> 00:00:45.790
And it covers detectors, fakes

18
00:00:45.790 --> 00:00:47.510
and how to report a problem.

19
00:00:48.740 --> 00:00:50.660
Approving a tool means asking

20
00:00:50.660 --> 00:00:51.940
a few questions first.

21
00:00:52.670 --> 00:00:54.950
Where does our data go, and is

22
00:00:54.950 --> 00:00:55.890
it used for training?

23
00:00:56.510 --> 00:00:57.630
What age does it allow?

24
00:00:58.400 --> 00:00:59.980
What can it do on its own?

25
00:01:00.670 --> 00:01:02.430
And who will look after it here?

26
00:01:03.740 --> 00:01:05.520
Here's what makes a policy hold.

27
00:01:06.230 --> 00:01:08.450
A rule people must remember will

28
00:01:08.450 --> 00:01:10.050
be forgotten on a busy day.

29
00:01:10.630 --> 00:01:11.650
A check the system

30
00:01:11.650 --> 00:01:13.370
enforces holds every day.

31
00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:17.100
So wherever you can, turn the rule into a

32
00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:20.340
setting, a permission or a line of code.

33
00:01:21.560 --> 00:01:23.980
Here's our send rule as code,

34
00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:26.160
with a bug we found in it.

35
00:01:26.710 --> 00:01:29.510
The first version sent a message if the

36
00:01:29.510 --> 00:01:32.750
agent asked or a teacher said yes, so

37
00:01:32.750 --> 00:01:34.010
the agent could approve itself.

38
00:01:34.610 --> 00:01:37.850
One word fixes it: and, instead of or.

39
00:01:38.530 --> 00:01:40.730
Now it sends only when both are

40
00:01:40.730 --> 00:01:42.430
true, every single time.

41
00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:44.080
Quick check!

42
00:01:44.690 --> 00:01:47.710
Two schools want the same thing: no

43
00:01:47.710 --> 00:01:50.370
messages to parents without a teacher's

44
00:01:50.370 --> 00:01:50.550
yes.

45
00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:52.740
Which rule holds?

46
00:01:54.220 --> 00:01:54.700
The second.

47
00:01:55.410 --> 00:01:57.230
When the bot simply can't send

48
00:01:57.230 --> 00:01:59.470
without a tap on Approve, the rule

49
00:01:59.470 --> 00:02:01.250
holds on the busiest day.

50
00:02:01.830 --> 00:02:04.330
Keep posters for habits only people can

51
00:02:04.330 --> 00:02:06.690
do, like taking names out of a prompt.

52
00:02:07.970 --> 00:02:10.650
Write the page in plain words, in the

53
00:02:10.650 --> 00:02:13.310
languages your staff, pupils and parents

54
00:02:13.310 --> 00:02:13.690
read.

55
00:02:14.330 --> 00:02:15.270
Check it against your

56
00:02:15.270 --> 00:02:16.950
country's data protection law.

57
00:02:17.530 --> 00:02:20.590
Then review it each term, and whenever a

58
00:02:20.590 --> 00:02:22.530
new tool or an incident arrives.

59
00:02:24.030 --> 00:02:26.210
In the app, pick a job and

60
00:02:26.210 --> 00:02:27.710
give an agent its tools.

61
00:02:28.410 --> 00:02:30.810
Run the attack set and count the damage.

62
00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:33.640
Then cut the tools to what the job

63
00:02:33.640 --> 00:02:36.640
needs and run it again: that list of

64
00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:38.620
tools is a line in your policy.

65
00:02:39.930 --> 00:02:40.690
Your turn.

66
00:02:41.310 --> 00:02:44.190
Our timetable agent only needs to read the

67
00:02:44.190 --> 00:02:46.830
timetable, but it was given email too.

68
00:02:47.420 --> 00:02:49.200
What should the policy say?

69
00:02:50.420 --> 00:02:52.780
Only the tools its job needs.

70
00:02:53.590 --> 00:02:56.550
Remember: one page in plain words,

71
00:02:57.130 --> 00:02:59.590
questions before a tool is approved,

72
00:03:00.190 --> 00:03:03.350
checks the system enforces, and a review

73
00:03:03.350 --> 00:03:03.990
each term.

74
00:03:05.150 --> 00:03:07.350
Try it at school this week: draft

75
00:03:07.350 --> 00:03:09.590
your one-page policy with two

76
00:03:09.590 --> 00:03:11.050
colleagues and two pupils.

77
00:03:11.810 --> 00:03:14.610
Mark each rule: something people remember,

78
00:03:15.090 --> 00:03:17.170
or something the system enforces.

79
00:03:17.850 --> 00:03:20.190
Then turn one rule into a

80
00:03:20.190 --> 00:03:20.950
setting this week.
